#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544869 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to point $B(1,2),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+(y-1)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{3}+1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{3}+1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+(y-1)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(y-1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(y-1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\cdot 1+({t}^{3}+1-1)\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+3{t}^{5}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{6}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544996 :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=2u-v,\\,y=3u+2,\\,z=u+2v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 5$ and $-1\\le v\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u-v,\\,3u+2,\\,u+2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u+2),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,3,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u+2),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-5,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨6,\\,-5,\\,3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+25+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{70}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}{∬}_{D}\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot 5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10 \\sqrt{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545007 :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨-u+v,\\,u-2v,\\,3v⟩$ that is subject to the constraint ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 20.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,3,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨3,\\,3,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 20$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 20},\n$$\n\nof radius $2 \\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{19}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot (2 \\sqrt{5})^{2}) \\\\  & =20 \\sqrt{19} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545014 :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e3f80c8a0975c70ee2990dc230af59ffb9f0944bdbdc86c6fb96f693ebdac29.png)\n\nWhich integral  expression gives the arc length of the curve $h(t)=(2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)})\\,i+(2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)})\\,j+(3 t)\\,k$ from $t=0$ to $t=2 \\pi?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $h'(t)$ and $\\|h'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(t) & =(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}))\\,i+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}))\\,j+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t))\\,k \\\\  & =(- 4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)})\\,i+(4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)})\\,j+(3)\\,k \\\\ \\|h'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^{2} + \\left(4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^{2} + 3^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 16 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\|h'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\sqrt{25}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545016 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=\\langle \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t},\\,2 t e^{2 t},\\,\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t}\\rangle\n$$ from $t=0$ to $t=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t e^{2 t}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t})\\rangle \\\\  & =\\langle \\left(- 6 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} + 2 \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t},\\,4 t e^{2 t} + 2 e^{2 t},\\,\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 6 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} + 2 \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t}\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(4 t e^{2 t} + 2 e^{2 t}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\left(- 6 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} + 2 \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 6 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} + 2 \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(16 t^{2} e^{4 t} + 16 t e^{4 t} + 4 e^{4 t}\\right) + \\left(16 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 64 e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 64 e^{4 t} \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) + \\left(64 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 64 e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 16 e^{4 t} \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(4 \\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2} + 80\\right) e^{4 t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 \\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2} + 80} e^{2 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}2 \\sqrt{\\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2} + 20} e^{2 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545025 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t)=\\langle 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)},\\,3 e^{2 t},\\,2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle$ from $t=-1$ to $t=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $f'(t)$ and $\\|f'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 e^{2 t}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)})\\rangle \\\\  & =\\langle - 4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)},\\,6 e^{2 t},\\,4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle \\\\ \\|f'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^{2} + \\left(4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^{2} + \\left(6 e^{2 t}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 32 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 16 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) + \\left(16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 32 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 16 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) + 36 e^{4 t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{32 \\left(\\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) + 36 e^{4 t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36 e^{4 t} + 32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{-1}^{1}\\|f'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{-1}^{1}2 \\sqrt{9 e^{4 t} + 8}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545026 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=\\langle \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t},\\,2 t e^{2 t},\\,\\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t}\\rangle\n$$ from $t=0$ to $t=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t e^{2 t}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t})\\rangle \\\\  & =\\langle \\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 6 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} + 2 \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t},\\,4 t e^{2 t} + 2 e^{2 t},\\,2 \\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} + \\left(6 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t}\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(4 t e^{2 t} + 2 e^{2 t}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 6 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} + 2 \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t}\\right)^{2} + \\left(2 \\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} + \\left(6 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(16 t^{2} e^{4 t} + 16 t e^{4 t} + 4 e^{4 t}\\right) + \\left(e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 16 e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 64 e^{4 t} \\cos^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) + \\left(64 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 16 e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} + e^{4 t} \\cos^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(4 \\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2} + 65\\right) e^{4 t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 \\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2} + 65} e^{2 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{4 \\left(2 t + 1\\right)^{2} + 65} e^{2 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545029 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=\\langle \\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t},\\,3 t e^{3 t},\\,\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t}\\rangle\n$$ from $t=0$ to $t=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t e^{3 t}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t})\\rangle \\\\  & =\\langle \\left(- 9 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} + 3 \\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t},\\,9 t e^{3 t} + 3 e^{3 t},\\,3 \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} + \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 9 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t}\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(9 t e^{3 t} + 3 e^{3 t}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\left(- 9 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} + 3 \\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t}\\right)^{2} + \\left(3 \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} + \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 9 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(81 t^{2} e^{6 t} + 54 t e^{6 t} + 9 e^{6 t}\\right) + \\left(36 e^{6 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 144 e^{6 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 144 e^{6 t} \\cos^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) + \\left(144 e^{6 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 144 e^{6 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 36 e^{6 t} \\cos^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(9 \\left(3 t + 1\\right)^{2} + 180\\right) e^{6 t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9 \\left(3 t + 1\\right)^{2} + 180} e^{3 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}3 \\sqrt{\\left(3 t + 1\\right)^{2} + 20} e^{3 t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545033 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t)=\\langle \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)},\\,4 e^{2 t},\\,\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\rangle$ from $t=-1$ to $t=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $f'(t)$ and $\\|f'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(4 e^{2 t}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)})\\rangle \\\\  & =\\langle - 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)},\\,8 e^{2 t},\\,3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\rangle \\\\ \\|f'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)^{2} + \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)^{2} + \\left(8 e^{2 t}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 18 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 9 \\cos^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) + \\left(9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 18 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 9 \\cos^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) + 64 e^{4 t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18 \\left(\\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) + 64 e^{4 t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64 e^{4 t} + 18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{-1}^{1}\\|f'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{2} \\sqrt{32 e^{4 t} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545038 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $r(t)=\\langle \\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t},\\,t e^{t},\\,\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}\\rangle$ from $t=0$ to $t=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t e^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t})\\rangle \\\\  & =\\langle \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t} + \\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t},\\,t e^{t} + e^{t},\\,\\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t} + \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(t e^{t} + e^{t}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t} + \\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t} + \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(t^{2} e^{2 t} + 2 t e^{2 t} + e^{2 t}\\right) + \\left(e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 9 e^{2 t} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + \\left(9 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + e^{2 t} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} + 10\\right) e^{2 t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} + 10} e^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} + 10} e^{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545057 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $r(t)=\\langle \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t},\\,t e^{t},\\,\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}\\rangle$ from $t=0$ to $t=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t e^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t})\\rangle \\\\  & =\\langle \\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t} + \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t},\\,t e^{t} + e^{t},\\,\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t} + \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}\\rangle \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(t e^{t} + e^{t}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t} + \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t} + \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(t^{2} e^{2 t} + 2 t e^{2 t} + e^{2 t}\\right) + \\left(e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 10 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 25 e^{2 t} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + \\left(25 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 10 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + e^{2 t} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} + 26\\right) e^{2 t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} + 26} e^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} + 26} e^{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545070 :question/problem "![](images/35/3500678bdca0125f1796c68ad54aab04c05ece23914f2bde0a0589119d291fc8.png)\n\nWhich integral  expression gives the arc length of the curve $g(t)=\\langle 4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2\\rangle$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=\\pi?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $g'(t)$ and $\\|g'(t)\\|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g'(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2)\\rangle \\\\  & =\\langle 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle \\\\ \\|g'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^{2} + \\left(4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(16 t^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 32 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 16 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + \\left(16 t^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 32 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\|g'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}4 \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545075 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 2 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le - 3 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le 3 z\\le 3\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 2 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le - 3 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le 3 z\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x,\\quad v=- 3 x + y,\\quad w=3 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 3\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{2},\\frac{3 u}{2} + v,\\frac{w}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{3}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{5}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(5\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(3\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545077 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 2 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le 2 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 4\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 2 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le 2 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x,\\quad v=2 x + y,\\quad w=2 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 4\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{2},- u + v,\\frac{w}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{5}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(5\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(4\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545098 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 3 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 2\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 3 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=3 x,\\quad v=x + y,\\quad w=2 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 2\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{3},- \\frac{u}{3} + v,\\frac{w}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{4}\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{5}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{4}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(5\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(4\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545101 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 2 x\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le - 2 x + y\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 2\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 2 x\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le - 2 x + y\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x,\\quad v=- 2 x + y,\\quad w=2 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 2\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{2},u + v,\\frac{w}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{6}\\int_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{6}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{6}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{5}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(6\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(5\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(6\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545103 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 2 x\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 6\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 2 x\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x,\\quad v=x + y,\\quad w=2 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 6\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{2},- \\frac{u}{2} + v,\\frac{w}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{3}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{6}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{3}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(6\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(3\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(6\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545111 :question/problem "![](images/e3/e3da6e664f55c8abb41ff05e3f7e7108ab1f7c648e887402885347eccd42ed46.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at the origin and has a radius of $3,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={3}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545132 :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4ca74438b4c5e5eeae2ce46d7a86964d74adfb1e12df6029a41a04c0dea7e93.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at $(- \\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{1}{2})$ and has a radius of $\\frac{3}{2},$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right))^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right))^{2}={\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}\\,⟹\\,(x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((y+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+\\frac{7}{2})+2(y+\\frac{1}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2(y+\\frac{1}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2(x+\\frac{7}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{1}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545146 :question/problem "![](images/3e/3ea6262a334d81630f0d8c1b35654c7d3c74745a344587be73b1852015ed3f47.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at $(- \\frac{7}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and has a radius of $\\frac{3}{2},$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right))^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}={\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}\\,⟹\\,(x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+\\frac{7}{2})+2(y-\\frac{1}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2(y-\\frac{1}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2(x+\\frac{7}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y-\\frac{1}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545147 :question/problem "![](images/a3/a335ec0ac7f4502b973f0ef444f8ff96f4bd630e4c2e957ef86e52f29776dfbc.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at $(- \\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{7}{2})$ and has a radius of $\\frac{3}{2},$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right))^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right))^{2}={\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}\\,⟹\\,(x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((y+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+\\frac{7}{2})+2(y+\\frac{7}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2(y+\\frac{7}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2(x+\\frac{7}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{7}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545152 :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c0144393116ae0092b650059486e3db9d998bd347adc8219dadc70b0d45a647.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at the origin and has a radius of $6,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={6}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(36) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545153 :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c68460c25eed3e9dfa3e52d62b8b5a460a615bf00f50954ab0a920e4892bcb0.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at the origin and has a radius of $1,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={1}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545172 :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b008917c1a1bc0f2109fd614d9470b4c805bb09dcad15dc50dafd7dc9b177ed.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at $(- \\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{5}{2})$ and has a radius of $\\frac{3}{2},$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right))^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right))^{2}={\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}\\,⟹\\,(x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+\\frac{7}{2})+2(y+\\frac{5}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2(y+\\frac{5}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2(x+\\frac{7}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{5}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545174 :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbff2d37121b1e6d9c8ba358ecb354a599e1c785dcd5fdde1d40e26937db6fb8.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at the origin and has a radius of $5,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={5}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(25) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545175 :question/problem "![](images/02/025ef38d69954bfcc7f2a463a97239e442c446803ede7fee6ee172b30a356cbd.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at $(- \\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{3}{2})$ and has a radius of $\\frac{3}{2},$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right))^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right))^{2}={\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}\\,⟹\\,(x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+\\frac{7}{2})+2(y+\\frac{3}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2(y+\\frac{3}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2(x+\\frac{7}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545177 :question/problem "![](images/38/38bd557f03e547ee2634d361fe1768cf8b2089a51230c2795e17036676b50bcd.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at $(2,1)$ and has a radius of $2,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}={2}^{2}\\,⟹\\,(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x-2)^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((y-1)^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2(x-2)+2(y-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2(y-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2(x-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x-2}{y-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546100 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=(-1)\\cdot (z)+(-2)\\cdot (x)+(3)\\cdot (y),$ where $x=t - 3 s$, $y=- s - t$, $z=- s + 2 t$. Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-2,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =-3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)+(3)\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =6 - 3 + 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546109 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=(-3)\\cdot (x)+(3)\\cdot (y)+(3)\\cdot (z),$ where $x=s + t$, $y=- t + 2 s$, $z=- 3 s + 2 t$. Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =2, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(-3)\\cdot (1)+(3)\\cdot (2)+(3)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3 + 6 - 9 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546113 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=(-3)\\cdot (y)+(-2)\\cdot (x)+(-2)\\cdot (z),$ where $x=t - 3 s$, $y=- t + 3 s$, $z=2 s + 2 t$. Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-2,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =-3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (3)+(-2)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =6 - 9 - 4 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546116 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=(-1)\\cdot (x)+(-1)\\cdot (z)+(2)\\cdot (y),$ where $x=t + 3 s$, $y=s - t$, $z=- 3 s + 2 t$. Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(-1)\\cdot (3)+(2)\\cdot (1)+(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3 + 2 + 3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546118 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=(-1)\\cdot (y)+(2)\\cdot (x)+(3)\\cdot (z),$ where $x=t - 3 s$, $y=- t - 2 s$, $z=- s + 2 t$. Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =-3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-2, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(2)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-6 + 2 - 3 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546147 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- The function $B(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $C(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, x^2, 1\\rangle$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546156 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to values in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to values in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546165 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $B(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, y^2, z\\rangle$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546177 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to values in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546189 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $B(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, y^2, z\\rangle$; $C(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, y^2, z\\rangle$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546196 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $C(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, x^2, 1\\rangle$; $B(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, x^2, 1\\rangle$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546198 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to values in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, y^2, z\\rangle$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546204 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ to a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $B(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- The function $C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x, x^2, 1\\rangle$ only."}]}
